# net80211 component options.

menu "net80211"

config WLAN_RX_BA
	bool "RX block-ack reorder window"
	help
	  Compiles the driver's RX block-ack reorder window (wlan_rx_ba.c) and
	  emits -DCONFIG_WLAN_RX_BA=1.  The old profiles set the make variable
	  and the same -D twice (once as an assignment, once inside
	  CONFIG_LEVERS); after the migration there is one source of truth.

config WLAN_STA_AUTHZ
	bool "station ASSOC -> AUTHORIZED transition after an open join"
	default n
	help
	  Send the driver the AUTHORIZED step of mac80211's station ladder
	  after an open association, and step back down to ASSOC on teardown.
	  mac80211 runs both immediately after ASSOC on an interface without a
	  control port (net/mac80211/mlme.c, sta_info.c).  A driver acts on it:
	  iwlwifi only lets the firmware pick a transmit rate for a station at
	  AUTHORIZED (mvm/tx.c sets TX_CMD_FLG_STA_RATE there), initialises
	  the firmware rate table for the station's links and re-sends the MAC
	  context.  Below AUTHORIZED it builds the rate from
	  info->control.rates[0], which the seam fills with idx 0 - CCK
	  1 Mbit/s on 2.4 GHz.  Off by default so no other line changes
	  shape; a profile that wants the parity step sets it to y.

config WLAN_TX_BA
	bool "TX block-ack state machine (port side)"
	default y
	help
	  Compiles the port's TX block-ack state machine (wlan_tx_ba.c) and
	  emits -DCONFIG_WLAN_TX_BA=1.  Off is a diagnostic arm, not a shape:
	  ieee80211_start_tx_ba_session() answers -EOPNOTSUPP, the driver never
	  sets RTW_TXQ_AMPDU, and every frame rides a single-frame URB.  Used by
	  W-AGG-02 to separate "aggregation is wrong" from "the frame never got
	  on the air" (aggregate arm) with one variable.

config WLAN_SEAM_TX_BACKLOG_MAX
	int "seam transmit burst bound (bulk-OUT transfers outstanding, 0 = off)"
	default 0
	range 0 64
	help
	  Refuse a data frame at the seam while the USB engine already holds
	  this many bulk-OUT transfers, queue plus in flight.  The chip's own
	  transmit queue is finite; on 2026-11-04 the 8821CU stopped completing
	  any bulk-OUT with 22 outstanding and the endpoint never moved again.
	  The bridge's stash carries the refused frame, so this is backpressure.

config WLAN_SEAM_TX_MIN_SPARE
	int "skb headers the seam keeps free when it admits a TX frame"
	default 48
	range 0 160
	help
	  The transmit seam and the receive path draw from one socket-buffer
	  pool.  A driver whose transmit path accepts work faster than its
	  transport can carry it (the USB substitute queues every frame the
	  stack hands down, and the engine has one transfer in flight per
	  endpoint) grows an unbounded tx backlog, and each queued frame pins
	  a header slot.  When the pool is empty the receive path can neither
	  re-arm its bulk-in URBs nor allocate a buffer for the next frame,
	  and a bulk-in driver that retries by re-queueing its own work turns
	  the shortage into a CPU livelock - the whole machine stops, console
	  included.

	  With this set to N, a data frame is admitted only while more than N
	  header slots (static slots plus heap headers) are free, so the
	  receive path always has room.  A refused frame takes the existing
	  seam-noskb route (the bridge's one-frame stash and its deferred
	  wake), which is backpressure, not a drop.  Management frames are
	  never gated.  0 disables the gate.

endmenu
